INITCTL(1)                  User Commands                   INITCTL(1)

NAME
    initctl - list and control the programs started by init

SYNOPSIS
    initctl [list]
    initctl status|start|stop|restart NAME
    initctl reload [FILE]
    initctl poweroff|reboot|halt

DESCRIPTION
    initctl sends one request to init over the abstract socket "init"
    and prints the reply. Without arguments, or with list, it prints
    one row per entry of init.conf(5): name, kind, state, pid, the
    number of starts, and a detail (the running time, the exit status
    of a failed entry, or the absent path of a skipped one).

    The states are waiting (not started yet, or due for a restart),
    running, stopping, stopped (by request, not restarted), done (a
    task that exited with status 0), failed (a task that failed, or a
    service given up on after five quick exits in a row) and skipped
    (the if= path does not exist).

COMMANDS
    status NAME   print the row of one entry
    start NAME    start a stopped, failed or skipped entry; a task is
                  run again and waited for
    stop NAME     send SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after three seconds, and
                  wait; the entry is not restarted. The console entry
                  cannot be stopped, only restarted
    restart NAME  stop, then start
    reload [FILE] read the configuration again (FILE instead of the
                  file read last): removed entries are stopped, new
                  ones started, changed commands apply at the next
                  start
    poweroff, reboot, halt
                  stop every entry in reverse order and shut the
                  system down, as shutdown(1), reboot(1) and halt(1)

EXIT STATUS
    0 when init answered ok, 1 with the diagnostic on standard error
    otherwise.

SEE ALSO
    init.conf(5), shutdown(1), ps(1)
